---
// An actual shell, not a picture of one. The commands read the same data
// files the rest of the site renders from, so the terminal cannot drift out
// of date with the sections beneath it.
//
// The seeded `fastfetch` output is rendered server-side, so the page is
// complete and readable at rest — with JavaScript off, or for a crawler,
// this is a normal <pre> full of real text.
import { projects, running, skillGroups } from '../data/site';
import { person, profile, personEs, profileEs } from '../data/cv.mjs';
import { skins } from '../data/skins.mjs';
import { ui, fastfetchIcons } from '../data/ui.mjs';
import { locales, pick } from '../data/i18n.mjs';

const { lang = 'en' } = Astro.props;
const t = ui[lang];
const name = person.name;
const location = lang === 'en' ? person.location : personEs.location;
const headline = lang === 'en' ? person.headline : personEs.headline;
const profileLine = lang === 'en' ? profile[1] : profileEs[1];
const CONTACT_LABEL_ES = { Email: 'Correo', Phone: 'Teléfono', Site: 'Sitio' };

// A tone-ramp rendering of Javier's photo, above the wordmark.
//
// Three things this needs that the earlier versions got wrong. The wall is
// flood-filled away from the borders, so luminance maps DIRECTLY — bright skin
// becomes dense glyphs. Inverting instead (to drop the wall for free) is what
// turned the face into a void inside a wall of ink. It is drawn at 88 columns,
// because likeness at this scale is mostly a resolution problem. And it gets
// its own font-size, PORTRAIT_EM below, so those columns occupy exactly the
// width of the 44-column wordmark underneath.
//
// The tone work is what makes the face legible rather than a wash: a global
// gamma drops the mid-tone hair back so it stops competing with the lit
// forehead; a black crush deepens the lenses without blowing out that
// forehead, which a global contrast curve would; and a local-contrast pass
// (large-radius high-pass, blended back toward the global image) gives the
// nose, mouth and beard their own contrast independent of the hair, since
// they all sit in one narrow mid-tone band that the downsample flattens
// further. The ramp deliberately has no leading space: with one, the
// faintest tone mapped to blank whatever the floor was, so the dark hair
// above the forehead never rendered and the head looked shaved. Blankness
// is the floor's job alone, which the background mask makes safe.
// The grid is generated at a cell aspect of 0.6, not the usual 0.5: JetBrains
// Mono's advance is 0.6em and .face sets line-height:1, so assuming a cell is
// twice as tall as it is wide produced too few rows and squashed the face
// vertically. The source is cropped to the shape the layout can give it
// rather than scaled into place, so what remains keeps its proportions.
// A final pass trims the source to the part that actually renders: the
// extreme left and right are dark hair that falls below the floor, and those
// blank columns strip off, which would otherwise leave the drawn portrait
// narrower than its crop. Regenerate with scratchpad/hero7.py.
